\section{Declaring names}

The chunk starts empty here; every extractor chapter adds its declarations.

<<required names>>=
# Declarations of the extractor chapters follow.
<<schema functions>>=
def requires(kind, attributes=(), relationships=()):
    """Declare that fbf reads these names of the JSON:API type `kind`."""
    REQUIRED.update(f"type {kind} attribute {name}" for name in attributes)
    REQUIRED.update(f"type {kind} relationship {name}"
                    for name in relationships)
@


\section{Checked access}

The accessors distinguish a missing name from a missing value.
An attribute that is present but [[null]] is normal (a comment without
upvotes, a deadline not set) and gives [[None]]; an attribute that is
absent from the resource means that FBF changed, and raises.
The message points to the recovery procedure, since that is what the reader
of the error needs next.

<<schema functions>>=
class SchemaError(Exception):
    """A resource lacks a name that fbf depends on."""

    def __init__(self, resource, kind, name):
        super().__init__(
            f"{resource.get('type')}: no {kind} {name!r}; FeedbackFruits may "
            "have changed its API, see the recovery procedure (fbf har-diff)")


def attribute(resource, name):
    """Return the attribute `name` of `resource`; raise `SchemaError`."""
    attributes = resource.get("attributes") or {}
    if name not in attributes:
        raise SchemaError(resource, "attribute", name)
    return attributes[name]


def relationship(resource, name):
    """Return the relationship `name` of `resource`; raise `SchemaError`."""
    relationships = resource.get("relationships") or {}
    if name not in relationships:
        raise SchemaError(resource, "relationship", name)
    return relationships[name]


def related_id(resource, name):
    """Return the id of the to-one relationship `name`, or `None`."""
    data = relationship(resource, name).get("data")
    return data["id"] if data else None
@

<<test functions>>=
import pytest

COMMENT = {"type": "comments", "id": "c",
           "attributes": {"body": "Hi", "upvotes-count": None},
           "relationships": {"user": {"data": {"type": "users", "id": "u"}},
                             "flag": {"data": None}}}

def test_attribute_distinguishes_null_from_missing():
  assert attribute(COMMENT, "body") == "Hi"
  assert attribute(COMMENT, "upvotes-count") is None
  with pytest.raises(SchemaError, match="comments: no attribute 'text'"):
    attribute(COMMENT, "text")

def test_related_id():
  assert related_id(COMMENT, "user") == "u"
  assert related_id(COMMENT, "flag") is None
  with pytest.raises(SchemaError):
    related_id(COMMENT, "author")

def test_requires_records_shape_lines():
  requires("test-type", attributes=["a"], relationships=["r"])
  assert {"type test-type attribute a",
          "type test-type relationship r"} <= REQUIRED
@


\section{The contract with FBF}

The declarations are only worth something if they are true of FBF.
The contract test checks them, one declared type at a time: it fetches an
example resource of the type and requires every declared name of that type
to be among the resource's attribute and relationship names.
It compares in the shape format, with [[type_records]] from \cref{HAR},
which turns a resource into exactly the records that [[requires]] makes.

How to get an example differs by type, so each chapter registers a fetcher
for the types it declares (\cref{Examples}).
A type without a fetcher fails the test, with a message that says what to
add: otherwise a new chapter could declare names that nothing ever checks.
That check comes before anything that needs FBF, so it fails offline too,
even where the recordings are missing and the rest of the test is skipped.

The test runs in both modes.
With [[--live]] it is the real contract test: it checks our declarations
against FBF as it is today.
In replay it checks them against the recording, which catches a mistyped
declaration at once, and a declaration added after the recording, until we
record again.
The assertion shows the missing records, which are names, never values.
<<test functions>>=
import conftest
from fbf.har import type_records

DECLARED_TYPES = sorted({record.split()[1] for record in REQUIRED})

@pytest.mark.vcr
@pytest.mark.parametrize("kind", DECLARED_TYPES)
def test_fbf_has_the_names_we_depend_on(kind, request):
  fetch = conftest.EXAMPLES.get(kind)
  if fetch is None:
    pytest.fail(f"no example of {kind!r} to check its names on; register "
                f"one with @example({kind!r}) in an @<<example fetchers>> "
                "chunk of the chapter that declares it", pytrace=False)
  fbf = request.getfixturevalue("fbf")
  resource = fetch(fbf, fbf.login)
  assert resource["type"] == kind
  declared = {record for record in REQUIRED if record.split()[1] == kind}
  missing = sorted(declared - set(type_records(resource)))
  assert missing == []
@
